.TH VELVET-CONFIG 1 2026-08-03 Velvet "Velvet Manual"
.SH NAME
velvet-config \- run the local Velvet Configurator
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ./config
.RI [ start | stop | version | help ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ./config
serves the Velvet Configurator from the repository it lives in. The
Configurator edits the same
.BR velvet.yml (5)
format the monitor reads and previews the real status page whilst you change
it.
.PP
This is the local Configurator. It reads and writes a file on your computer and
opens no network connection, so it knows about no installation and reports no
installed release rather than one it cannot check. The hosted Configurator at
.UR https://setup.velvet.li/configurator/
setup.velvet.li/configurator
.UE
is the one that signs in with GitHub and can update an installation.
.PP
The command takes exactly one argument, and running it with none is the same as
running
.BR help .
Any other number of arguments, or an unrecognised one, is an error.
.PP
The server binds
.B 127.0.0.1
on port
.BR 2342 ,
so it is reachable from the machine it runs on and from nowhere else. The
address is fixed, which means two checkouts cannot serve at the same time.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B start
Start the server in the background and open
.B http://127.0.0.1:2342
in the default browser. When a server started from this same checkout is
already running, the browser is opened again and no second server is started.
Building the Configurator assets first is a prerequisite, and the command says
so rather than serving an empty directory.
.TP
.B stop
Stop the server started from this checkout. It reports that nothing was running
rather than failing when there is no server to stop, and it refuses to stop a
server it did not start.
.TP
.B version
Print the revision of the working tree, taken from the nearest tag, the commit,
and whether the tree is modified. Outside a Git checkout it prints
.BR development .
.TP
.B help
Print the usage summary and the address the Configurator is served at.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B VELVET_CONFIG_NO_OPEN
Set to
.B 1
to start the server without opening a browser. Intended for automated runs and
for machines with no desktop browser.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ./config
The command itself, at the root of a Velvet checkout.
.TP
.I ./configurator/
The built Configurator assets the server serves. They are produced by the
repository's own build and the server refuses to start without them.
.TP
.IR $TMPDIR/velvet-configurator- <id> /server.json
The address, process, and access token of the running server, readable only by
its owner. The
.I id
is derived from the path of the checkout, so each checkout tracks its own
server. The file is removed when the server stops.
.TP
.IR $TMPDIR/velvet-configurator- <id> /server.log
Output of the background server, which is where a failure to start is
explained.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The command completed.
.TP
.B 1
The arguments were rejected, the assets were missing, or the server failed to
start or to stop cleanly. Rejected arguments print the usage summary as well.
.SH EXAMPLES
Start the Configurator and open it:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
\&./config start
.fi
.RE
.PP
Start it on a machine with no browser:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
VELVET_CONFIG_NO_OPEN=1 ./config start
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR velvet (7),
.BR velvet.yml (5)
